  "final": "All checks pass. Summary of the change:\n\n**New module `src/exporter.py`** — the single shared entry point:\n- `export_rows(rows, format)` dispatches `'csv'` and `'json'` to internal serializers and raises `ValueError(\"unsupported format\")` for anything else.\n- `_normalize(rows)` is a generator that consumes `rows` exactly once (so one-shot iterables like generators work), never mutates the input, preserves order, keeps only `id` and `name`, maps missing keys or `None` to `''`, and stringifies other values. It yields each row both as a cell list (for CSV) and as a dict (for JSON), so normalization runs in one place for both formats.\n- CSV: `csv.writer` on `StringIO(newline=\"\")` with `lineterminator=\"\\n\"` — LF endings, standard quoting, and the `id,name` header written unconditionally (even with zero rows).\n- JSON: `json.dumps(..., ensure_ascii=False, separators=(\",\", \":\"))`.\n\n**Simplified public wrappers** — `src/csv_exporter.py.export_csv(rows)` and `src/json_exporter.py.export_json(rows)` are now one-line delegates to `export_rows`; their duplicated normalization and serialization loops are gone. Output strings are byte-for-byte identical to before.\n\n**`src/dispatcher.py`** is untouched and keeps working (its `export(rows, format)` simply routes to the wrappers, which now share the pipeline).\n\n**Verification**: the original `checks/public.py` passes unchanged, and a new `checks/exporter.py` covers the empty-row CSV header, LF endings and standard quoting, JSON separators/`ensure_ascii=False`, stringification and `''` for missing/`None` values, `ValueError` for unsupported formats, one-shot generator input, no input mutation, and wrapper/entry-point agreement.",
